    "id": "105",
    "title": "Resonance RLC Circuit",
    "excerpt": "If we look a vanilla [[RLC circuits]] , it's clear that energy is going to decay, because the [[Resistors]] is going to transform that energy from the...",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\ndate: 2025-12-13 11:16\nmodified: 2025-12-13 11:35\n---\nIf we look a vanilla [[RLC circuits]] , it's clear that energy is going to decay, because the [[Resistors]] is going to transform that energy from the [[Electromotive Force]] into heat. If we don't want that happens then we can add a [[Alternating Current]] to fight against that loose of energy. \n\nThe **AC** frequency can't be any value, but a specific one such that we can consider that the resistor is not there. \n\nNo the definition is when the $I_{\\text{RMS}}$ is maximum.",
    "uploadDate": "2025-12-13 11:16",
