    "id": "151",
    "title": "Charges In A Magnetic Field",
    "excerpt": "If the [[Charge]] enter completely perpendicular to a [[Magnetic Field]] $\\vec{B}$, then is going to make circles, what is the radius? Well doing some...",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\ndate: 2025-11-21 07:55\nmodified: 2025-11-21 08:18\n---\nIf the [[Charge]] enter completely perpendicular to a [[Magnetic Field]] $\\vec{B}$, then is going to make circles, what is the radius? Well doing some classical mechanics.\n\n$$\nF=ma=\\frac{mv^{2}}{R}=qvB\n$$\nThen:\n$$\n\\frac{mv}{qB}=R\n$$\nNow if the particle don't enter perfectly perpendicular, we can decompose that velocity in two components, thus the another is perfectly aligned with the field and there is not a [[Magnetic Force]] that it's generated.\n\nBut that velocity is going to make that the particles make a helicoidal movement. And to find the $d$ just make the trivial $v\\times t$ . Where the $t$ can be find it via $T$ and $\\omega$ the [[Angular velocity]]. I mean is a [[Circular Motion]].\n\n",
    "uploadDate": "2025-11-21 07:55",
    "readTime": "5 min read",
    "fileName": "Charges in a Magnetic Field.md",
    "featured": false
