    "excerpt": "So you can write you [[Partial Differential Equation, what are]], like follow:",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\ndate: 2025-11-10 14:00\nmodified: 2025-11-10 14:21\n---\nSo you can write you [[Partial Differential Equation, what are]], like follow:\n\n$$ V-iR-\\frac{q}{C}=0 $$\n\nFrom it, you obtain: This assumes that you begin with charge equals zero, is a specific case, more generally you integrate on another limits.\n$$ q(t)=VC(1-e^{ -t/RC }) $$\n\nYou can obtain the [[Current Intensity]] by deriving, easily:\n\nNow if you want say goodbye to the charges: Because you already have charge that induce [[Potential (Voltage)]]\n\n$$\n\\frac{q}{C}-IR=0\n$$\n\nYou obtain that:\n\n$$\nq(t)=Qe^{-t/RC}\n$$",
