    "title": "Electric Field Dipole",
    "excerpt": "- So the electrical field produce when is very far away (basically we consider a punctual).",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\ndate: 2025-10-20 12:38\nmodified: 2025-10-20 12:50\n---\n- So the electrical field produce when is very far away (basically we consider a punctual).\n When $d\\ll r$, $r$ represent the distance from the **origin** and is on the same line that the [[Electric dipole]].\n$$\n\\vec{E}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\varepsilon_{0}} \\frac{2\\vec{\\rho}}{r^{3}}\n$$\nOn the line perpendicular line.\n$$\n\\vec{E}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\varepsilon_{0}}\\frac{-\\vec{\\rho}}{r^{3}}\n$$\nNow we can calculate in any point of the space.\n\n$$\n\\vec{E}=k\\left( 3\\frac{\\langle \\vec{\\rho},\\vec{r} \\rangle \\vec{r}}{r^{5}} -\\frac{\\vec{\\rho}}{r^{3}}\\right)\n$$\n\n- And the the cases when we want to calculate the [[Electric Field]] produced for this systems, where we construct the other are most fundamental and easy to solve, when the point where you want is in the line that the two charges trace and when is perpendicular. \n- \nThis don't contract to a single point!",
