    "excerpt": "You can shown that when you have a [[Electric Field]] $\\vec{E}$ and a [[Electric dipole]] the [[torque vector]] is:",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\nauthor: Jorge\ndate: 2025-10-01 08:13\nmodified: 2025-10-01 08:44\n---\nYou can shown that when you have a [[Electric Field]] $\\vec{E}$ and a [[Electric dipole]] the [[torque vector]] is:\n\n$$\n\\vec{\\tau}=\\vec{\\rho}\\times \\vec{E}\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector $\\vec{\\rho}$ is always trying to get line with the direction of $\\vec{E}$.\nSo that means that the electric field is going to apply a work.\n\nwhich is qeual to:\n\n$$\nW=\\rho E(\\cos \\phi-\\cos \\phi_{0})\n$$\n\nBut wait the works of a conservarive force is realted to the change of some Potentia field! [[conservative force physics]]. \n\nYou shown that the [[potential energy on electrostatic arrangments]] is:\n\n$$\nU=-\\vec{\\rho}\\cdot \\vec{E}\n$$\n\n",
