    "excerpt": "Now the important is that in the boundary of your [[Differential Equation]] you impose a function, such that the function evaluated on the [[boundary ...",
    "content": "---\ntags:\n  - baby\nauthor: Jorge\ndate: 2025-09-16 11:27\nmodified: 2025-09-16 11:40\n---\nNow the important is that in the boundary of your [[Differential Equation]] you impose a function, such that the function evaluated on the [[boundary concept for differential equations]]that you are searching is fixed to that point.\n\nOne way to describe is, let $f$ be the function so:\n\n$$\nf(x_{\\text{boundary}},y,z)=g(t)\n$$\n\nI mean if you want to generalize this is hard.",
